Allow response files to be special files.

Nixpkgs' compiler wrappers pass the flags they inject through a response file
written with a process substitution, so the driver is invoked as
`swiftc @/dev/fd/63` — a pipe, not a regular file. Two separate things in
swift-driver reject that:

  * `resolveResponseFile` requires `isFile`, which is false for a pipe. This
    half is https://github.com/swiftlang/swift-driver/pull/1197, open since
    2022 and still unmerged.

  * Even with that fixed, the read itself fails. `TSCBasic`'s
    `readFileContents` was reimplemented on top of Foundation's
    `Data(contentsOf:)`, which refuses anything that is not a regular file, so
    every invocation dies with

      error: FileSystemError(kind: TSCBasic.FileSystemError.Kind.invalidAccess,
        path: Optional(<AbsolutePath:"/dev/fd/63">), ...)

    That reads as a permissions problem but is not one — the same file is read
    happily by the C++ driver, which uses plain `open`/`read`. Fall back to a
    read that works on pipes when the regular-file read fails, so the
    behaviour is unchanged for ordinary response files.

